speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, some time ago I tabled an explanatory tabular statement of the tariff changes, of which I think every hon. member has a copy. If hon. gentlemen will now turn to page 21 of that statement they will find printed opposite each item the answers to a number of questions which I feel sure they otherwise would ask. This information is printed for the purpose of expediting the consideration of each item. I have, of course, further information in connection with each item; but as far as comparative rates are concerned, they are fully set out in the tabular statement commencing on page 21 with respect to schedule 1. I believe we are now on the second item; one item was carried.
Customs tariff-7. Meats, fresh, n.o.p.: ex (a) Edible offal of beef and veal, 4 cents per pound;
ex (c) Pork, li cents per pound.

John Lickman,
and a good Conservative, too. I received the following letter from him dated at Essex, April 8, 1938:
The enclosed resolution was unanimously passed at a meeting of corn growers and dealers held in Essex Wednesday night.
Copies have been forwarded to Hon. W. D. Euler, Hon. J. G. Gardiner, Hon. Chas. Dunning,, and Hon. J. L. llsley.
I would very much appreciate your assistance in order that the resolution may be given full consideration by the government.
The resolution reads as follows:
Whereas the present tariff structure between Canada, United States, and the British Empire is about to be adjusted, and whereas it is understood that the United States is making strong representations to have United States corn given free entry into Canada;
And whereas the present tariff of twenty cents a bushel against corn from all sources except South Africa is acknowledged to be a very high protection considering the size of the industry to be protected, and the comparative size of the corn consuming industry;
And whereas the cereal and corn starch industries enjoy the privilege of duty free corn as raw material and at the same time a very high protection on their finished product;
And whereas a considerable percentage of the total volume of corn imported free of duty by these industries is sold as offal for feed in a protected market, thus giving those industries a very unfair advantage;
And whereas the present situation with a tariff operative only when the Union of South Africa has no corn for export, has not been satisfactory in that the situation is very unstable and has made market conditions difficult to predict;
And whereas this uncertain situation has a disturbing effect on all grain markets throughout Canada;
And whereas if United States corn were given entirely free entry, a very valuable market in eastern Canada for western Canadian feed grain would be largely lost to cheap American corn, in years when United States has a large crop;
And whereas the distilling industry is known to be very profitable and could well afford to pay a slightly larger duty on the corn they have to import;
1996	COMMONS
Canada-United States Trade Agreement
Therefore be it resolved that the government be asked to place a stable duty of 10 cents a bushel against all corn entering Canada for all purposes and from all sources.
That is signed by the president of the Essex county farm bureau, a representative of the elevator association. I might add that the Essex county farm bureau is a representative organization, non-political, and one of the members of that organization, at the last Conservative convention held in Kingsville a short time ago, was a proposed Conservative candidate to contest the next federal election.

Mr. Chairman, this is
an item which I am afraid will fall rather heavily on production in British Columbia. I understand that the production of that province is in the neighbourhood of 40,000 tons, of which rather more than half is consumed in the coast markets, and the . balance, with the exception of some 100 or 1:50 cars which find their way eastward across the mountains, is consumed in British Columbia, a market in which our growers have received a reasonable price consequent upon a tariff of $15 a ton on potatoes coming in from the south. Now they appear to think that the inroads which will be made by potatoes from the south will be very considerable. Federal assistance in various forms which is given to producers in the south has so far had the tendency, in almost all the commodities to which it applies, to increase the acreage, and California potatoes, for instance, are at the top of their peak. There will definitely be a tendency for these potatoes to come into the British Columbia and Alberta and Saskatchewan markets, which of course will not be an assistance to the British Columbia producers.
There have been a great many resolutions on the subject, and I am not going to bother the minister at this time with sheets of them. I know representations have been made by the British Columbia coast marketing board to the Minister of National Defence, and representations no doubt were made by him to the government. But in this regard I want to put two short resolutions on Hansard. One is from the British Columbia coast vegetable marketing board:
Whereas the time limit for potatoes entering Canada on payment of duty is from June 15 to July 31 of each year;
And whereas this time limit will be of no value to the growers of early potatoes in the coast area of British Columbia;
Be it therefore resolved that recommendations be made that this time limit shall be from the 25th dav of May to the 15th day of July in each year.
They want the period drawn forward. In British Columbia there are what might almost be called two early crops. The period mentioned in the agreement is rather too late for the first and too early for the second, and the producers consider that they will be in grave difficulty consequent on the long free period. All through tl>eir resolutions and statements one finds the idea that in their opinion this should have been looked upon as a matter in which the countervailing principle should be adopted.

The second resolution I want to read is as follows:
"Whereas the Canada-United States treaty signed at Washington on November 17, 1938, appears very detrimental to the potato growers of British Columbia;
And whereas potatoes being shipped into Canada are duty free except for the period from June 15 to July 31 while potatoes from Canada entering the United States must pay duty of from $7.50 to $12 a ton;
And whereas this discriminates very unfairly against British Columbia growers;
Therefore be it resolved that the executive of this association protest most vigorously against this discrimination and request that arrangements between the two countries should be reciprocal, and if no protection can be given the duty should be countervailing; and further demand that regulations regarding grading and freedom from disease be strictly enforced on all importations.
So that the growers of potatoes in British Columbia are afraid that this is going to be a serious blow to them, and they do not see that the government of Canada has made any provision whatsoever to take care of dumping, which they look upon as almost inevitable.
They further draw attention to the fact that the United Kingdom, in its arrangements with the United States, has taken precautions should such a difficulty arise. Here I should like to read a quotation from the journal of the ministry of agriculture of the United Kingdom, of December, 1938:
A comprehensive trade agreement between the United States and the United Kingdom was signed on November 17, 1938. The surrender of advantages at present enjoyed by agriculture in the United Kingdom is not of a very serious nature. Among the general provisions of the agreement there are clauses that enable action to be taken to impose additional duties if any scheduled products are dumped or are subsidized upon exportation, and there are other clauses which provide freedom of action for the institution of quantitative regulation of or other measures operating under government authority which are designed to control production, market supply, quality or price of the domestic article.
Can the minister give any hope to these growers that in the event of dumping, since the duty has been entirely removed with the exception of a period of six weeks when in their opinion it will not help them, there is anything the Canadian government can do to relieve the situation?

Just one word with
regard to the seriousness of this situation in my constituency. For the moment I am dealing with it not so much in regard to the grower as in regard to the labour employed. It is going to be a very serious question where there is a very large acreage in tomatoes. I am not speaking only of the early tomatoes;
I am speaking of the main crop. We are fearful that the labour which hitherto has found a livelihood for a short time in the summer and fall, which enabled it to provide for winter sustenance, will be seriously affected.

Mr. Speaker, we are considering Bill No. 36, to amend the Prairie Farm Rehabilitation Act. I was surprised a few moments ago to hear the minister intimate that this is a comparatively unimportant bill, that it does not touch the principle of the act. The other day when the resolution was before the house the minister made a similar statement, and he used this sentence, which he more or less repeats to-day, as indicative of the main purpose of the bill:
Instead of basing our action on order in council, based on regulation, we are asking for legislative authority to do it by order in council.
In the first place, the language used is meaningless; it does not represent at all what the bill does. When I read the bill over I recognized at once that it was a serious departure. I obtained a copy of the original act and the amending act of 1937, and I invite the house to consider exactly what it is doing to-night. In presenting this view I do not wish to imply that I am objecting to the government doing anything that is possible for rehabilitation of the area in the west which was devastated, and assisting in any alleviation of the general farm conditions. What I do want the house to understand, and particularly the Liberal party, is the exent to which in this bill, as in one or two other measures that have already passed, we are departing from the normal democratic parliamentary procedure.
Let us look at the original act which the minister says we are not departing from in principle. It was passed in 1935, and I would ask the house to note that it was what might be termed an emergency measure; it was in recognition of extraordinary conditions in the west, and, very properly, steps were taken to provide for them. For the purpose of my argument, however, I wish to lay it down that it was an emergency measure. One of the main points in the bill was in section 3,
which provided for the setting up of an advisory committee, and I want the house to note the constitution of that committee. I quote:
(2)	One of the members of the committee shall be appointed chairman by the governor in council.
(3)	The committee shall consist of the following:
(a)	one representative of the Manitoba Grain Growing Farmers from the drought and soil drifting areas;
(b)	one representative of the Saskatchewan grain growing farmers from the drought and soil drifting areas;
(c)	one representative of the Alberta grain growing farmers from the drought and soil drifting areas;
(d)	one representative of Saskatchewan livestock farmers from the drought areas;
(e)	one representative of Alberta range farmers from the drought areas;
(f)	one representative of Mortgage Companies of Canada;
(g)	one representative of the Canadian Bankers' Association;
(h)	one representative each from the Canadian Pacific Railway Company and the Canadian National Railways;
(i)	two representatives from the dominion Department of Agriculture; and
(j)	one representative of the government in each of the provinces of Manitoba, Saskatchewan and Alberta.
It will be at once recognized that parliament at that time set up that committee as an advisory body, realizing that it was doing an unusual thing in spending money in the way it was; otherwise an appropriation would have been made in the estimates and the whole matter discussed here. But knowing that there was an emergency, and realizing that there was need to expedite expenditure,, they set up this non-partisan, independent committee representing the farmers, the cattle producers, the loan companies and the governments of the west, as a committee advisory to the minister. That stood for two years. Two years ago the minister came to parliament and he had section 3, to which I have referred, repealed. In place of that, the following was done:
The governor in council may establish one or more advisory committees to be known as prairie farm rehabilitation committees. . . .
That is the plural note. The first was a strong representative advisory committee.
